{"supervised_evaluation_methods": "Supervised evaluation methods are techniques used to assess the performance of segmentation algorithms by comparing the results with a manually segmented reference image, often considered as a gold standard or ground truth.|Here is another description: Supervised evaluation methods, also known as relative evaluation methods or empirical difference methods, are techniques used to assess the performance of algorithms by comparing their outputs to a predefined standard.|Here is another description: Supervised evaluation methods, also known as relative evaluation methods or empirical difference methods, are techniques used to assess the performance of segmentation algorithms.|Here is another description: Supervised evaluation methods refer to techniques that utilize labeled data to assess the performance of algorithms or models, particularly in the context of identifying and mitigating over-segmentation errors in image processing."},
