{"experiments": "The experiments refer to a series of tests conducted to assess the effectiveness of image segmentation techniques. These tests are crucial for determining the quality of segmentation algorithms and for selecting the optimal parameters for image processing.|Here is another description: The experiments refer to a series of tests or trials conducted to assess and compare various evaluation methods in the context of image segmentation. These experiments are specifically tailored to explore the effectiveness of different indicators.|Here is another description: The experiments refer to a series of tests or trials conducted to evaluate and compare different indicators in a detailed manner.|Here is another description: The experiments refer to a series of tests or studies conducted to evaluate the performance of various indicators, including newer ones like _Qp_, F-score, and Precision-recall.|Here is another description: The experiments refer to a series of tests or trials conducted to evaluate the performance of certain indicators. These experiments are part of a broader study that includes both detailed and brief descriptions of various indicators.|Here is another description: The experiments refer to a series of tests or trials conducted to evaluate the performance of various supervised indicators, including newer ones like _Qp_, F-score, and Precision-recall.|Here is another description: Experiments refer to the practical tests and trials conducted to assess the effectiveness and accuracy of image segmentation methods. These experiments are crucial for validating the performance of different segmentation techniques across various applications."},
{"experiments__evaluate__two_different_types_of_assessment_indicators_on_different_images": "The predicate 'evaluate' signifies the action of assessing or measuring the performance of the segmentation algorithms. This evaluation is essential for distinguishing between different algorithms and for fine-tuning their parameters based on the results obtained."},
