{"hd": "HD, or Hausdorff Distance, is a metric used to measure the distance between two sets of points. In the context of image segmentation evaluation, it is used to assess the similarity between segmented regions and ground truth data."},
{"hd__is_a__spatial_distance_based_metric": "\"is a\" serves as a classification predicate, indicating that HD belongs to a specific category of metrics. In this case, it categorizes HD as a spatial distance based metric, highlighting its role in measuring distances within spatial contexts."},
{"spatial_distance_based_metric": "Spatial distance based metrics are a category of evaluation metrics that measure the distance between points or regions in a spatial context. These metrics are crucial in assessing the accuracy of image segmentation algorithms by quantifying the spatial discrepancies between segmented regions and ground truth data.|Here is another description: Spatial distance based metrics are a class of evaluation metrics used in image segmentation to measure the distance or difference between segmented regions and a ground truth. These metrics help in assessing how well the segmentation algorithm performs in terms of spatial accuracy.|Here is another description: Spatial distance based metrics are a category of evaluation metrics that measure the distance between segmented regions and the ground truth in image segmentation tasks. Examples include Hausdorff Distance (HD), Average Symmetric Surface Distance (AVD), and Mean Hausdorff Distance (MHD)."},
