{"remote_sensing_image_segmentation_quality_evaluation_indicators": "Remote sensing image segmentation quality evaluation indicators are metrics or criteria used to assess the effectiveness and accuracy of segmentation algorithms applied to remote sensing images. These indicators help in evaluating how well the segmentation process has divided an image into meaningful segments or regions."},
{"remote_sensing_image_segmentation_quality_evaluation_indicators__are_based_on__comparison_of_global_and_local_feature_consistency_and_the_improvement_of_over_or_under_segmentation": "The phrase \"are based on\" indicates that the remote sensing image segmentation quality evaluation indicators rely on or are derived from certain principles or methods. In this context, it means that the indicators are grounded in specific approaches or criteria for assessing segmentation quality."},
{"comparison_of_global_and_local_feature_consistency_and_the_improvement_of_over_or_under_segmentation": "The comparison of global and local feature consistency and the improvement of over or under-segmentation refers to methods used to evaluate the quality of image segmentation. Global feature consistency looks at the overall coherence of features across the entire image, while local feature consistency focuses on the consistency within smaller, specific regions. Over-segmentation occurs when an image is divided into too many segments, and under-segmentation happens when it is divided into too few. Improving these issues is crucial for accurate segmentation."},
