{"analyzed_and_executed_simultaneously": "Analyzed and executed simultaneously refers to the capability of performing two tasks at the same time: evaluating the performance or characteristics of the segmentation algorithm and running the algorithm on image data. This dual-action approach aims to improve efficiency and reduce processing time."},
{"accuracy_of_the_analysis_results": "The accuracy of the analysis results refers to the correctness and reliability of the outcomes produced by the segmentation algorithms within the automatic detection system. It is a critical measure of how well the algorithms perform in segmenting images accurately."},
{"accuracy_of_the_analysis_results__can_be__evaluated": "The phrase \"can be\" indicates a possibility or capability. In this context, it suggests that there is a method or process available to assess or measure the accuracy of the analysis results produced by the segmentation algorithms."},
{"evaluated": "The evaluation of the accuracy of the analysis results involves assessing how well the segmentation algorithms perform in terms of correctness and reliability. This process may include comparing the outcomes against known standards or using specific metrics to quantify the performance."},
{"cpu_usage_and_processing_time": "CPU usage and processing time refer to the amount of computational resources and time required by a system to perform a specific task, in this case, the execution of segmentation algorithms."},
{"cpu_usage_and_processing_time__is__reduced_significantly": "The phrase \"is reduced significantly\" indicates a notable decrease in the amount of CPU usage and processing time required when using the parallelized automatic detection system compared to executing algorithms sequentially."},
{"reduced_significantly": "The reduction in CPU usage and processing time signifies an improvement in system efficiency and performance, allowing for quicker and more resource-efficient execution of segmentation algorithms."},
{"current_segmentation_assessment_metrics": "Current segmentation assessment metrics refer to the methods and criteria used to evaluate the performance of image segmentation algorithms. These metrics are crucial in determining how well a segmentation algorithm can delineate objects within an image."},
{"current_segmentation_assessment_metrics__focus_on__globally_valid_assessments": "The phrase \"focus on\" indicates that the current segmentation assessment metrics primarily concentrate their evaluation efforts on a specific type of assessment. This focus directs the metrics towards certain aspects of segmentation performance while potentially overlooking others."},
{"globally_valid_assessments": "Globally valid assessments in the context of image segmentation refer to evaluation methods that consider the overall performance of a segmentation algorithm across an entire image. These assessments aim to provide a comprehensive measure of how well the algorithm segments all objects within the image, without delving into local or region-specific errors."},
{"these_methods__are_ineffective_for__two_segmentation_results_with_very_similar_overall_performance_and_very_different_local_errors": "The phrase \"are ineffective for\" indicates that the current segmentation assessment metrics do not perform well or are not suitable in certain situations."},
{"two_segmentation_results_with_very_similar_overall_performance_and_very_different_local_errors": "The situation in question involves two segmentation results that have very similar overall performance but exhibit very different local errors. This means that while the general effectiveness of the segmentations may appear comparable, the specific, localized inaccuracies within each segmentation vary significantly."},
{"su_and_zhang_2017": "Su and Zhang (2017) are researchers who contributed to the field of image segmentation by developing a novel method for assessing segmentation errors."},
{"su_and_zhang_2017__proposed__a_method_for_local_and_global_quantization_segmentation_error": "The predicate \"proposed\" indicates that Su and Zhang (2017) put forward or suggested a new method or approach to address a specific problem in image segmentation."},
{"a_method_for_local_and_global_quantization_segmentation_error": "The method for local and global quantization segmentation error proposed by Su and Zhang (2017) is a technique designed to evaluate segmentation errors both locally and globally. It quantifies over-segmentation and under-segmentation errors using the degree of region overlap and generates an error map to depict local indicators, with a global indicator obtained through region-weighted summation of error values."},
{"over_segmentation_and_under_segmentation_errors": "Over-segmentation and under-segmentation errors refer to the inaccuracies in image segmentation where regions are either divided into too many segments or too few, respectively. These errors are critical in evaluating the performance of segmentation algorithms, as they directly impact the precision and effectiveness of the segmentation process."},
{"over_segmentation_and_under_segmentation_errors__are_quantized_using__the_degree_of_region_overlap": "The phrase \"are quantized using\" indicates a process where the over-segmentation and under-segmentation errors are measured and converted into discrete values. This quantification allows for a more precise and systematic evaluation of segmentation performance, enabling comparisons and improvements in algorithm efficiency."},
{"the_degree_of_region_overlap": "The degree of region overlap is a metric used to quantify the extent to which segmented regions align with the true regions in an image. This metric is crucial for assessing local and global segmentation errors, providing a detailed insight into the accuracy and reliability of segmentation algorithms."},
{"segmentation_error_map": "The segmentation error map is a visual representation generated from quantized error values that reflect the discrepancies between segmentation results and reference segmentation objects. It is designed to provide a detailed view of segmentation performance at a local level."},
{"segmentation_error_map__can_effectively_depict__local_indicators": "The phrase \"can effectively depict\" indicates that the segmentation error map has the capability to clearly and accurately represent or illustrate something. In this context, it means that the map is able to show detailed information about local segmentation errors."},
{"local_indicators": "Local indicators refer to specific, detailed metrics or measurements that assess the performance of segmentation algorithms on a localized basis within an image. These indicators help in identifying areas where the segmentation algorithm performs well or poorly at a granular level."},
{"global_indicator": "The global indicator is a comprehensive measure used to assess the overall performance of segmentation algorithms. It provides a single value that summarizes the effectiveness of a segmentation process across an entire image or dataset."},
{"global_indicator__can_be_obtained_by__region_weighted_summation_of_the_error_values_of_all_reference_segmentation_objects": "The phrase \"can be obtained by\" indicates the method or process through which the global indicator is derived. In this context, it specifies that the global indicator is calculated using a particular technique involving the aggregation of error values."},
{"region_weighted_summation_of_the_error_values_of_all_reference_segmentation_objects": "The region-weighted summation of the error values of all reference segmentation objects is a method where each segmentation object's error is weighted according to its region size or significance. These weighted errors are then summed to produce a single global indicator value, reflecting the overall segmentation quality."},
