{"sensitivity": "Sensitivity, also known as True Positive Rate (TPR) or Recall, is a critical metric in evaluating classification models. It measures the proportion of actual positives correctly identified by the model, indicating its ability to detect true positive instances. This metric is particularly important in imbalanced datasets and scenarios where false negatives are costly. In image segmentation, sensitivity assesses the algorithm's ability to correctly identify and delineate regions of interest within an image."},
{"recall__is_also_known_as__tpr": "The predicate \"is also known as\" indicates an alternative name or synonymous term for the subject. In this context, it signifies that Recall has another commonly used term, TPR, which is used interchangeably in the field of evaluation metrics for classification models."},
{"tpr": "TPR, or True Positive Rate, is another term for Recall in the context of classification model evaluation. It represents the ratio of true positive predictions to the total actual positives, providing insight into the model's ability to identify positive instances correctly."},
{"specificity": "Specificity is a statistical measure used in the context of binary classification to describe the proportion of negatives that are correctly identified. It is an important metric in evaluating the performance of a diagnostic test or a classification model."},
