{"the_evaluation_of_image_segmentation_results_more_accurate": "The evaluation of image segmentation results more accurate refers to the process of assessing the quality of image segmentation, which becomes more precise and reliable due to the application of this method."},
{"yang_et_al": "Yang et al. refers to a group of researchers who contributed to the field of image segmentation evaluation. Their work is noted for introducing innovative methods to assess the accuracy of image segmentation results."},
{"yang_et_al__proposed__a_new_discrepancy_measure_of_segmentation_evaluation_index_sei": "The term \"proposed\" indicates that Yang et al. put forward or suggested a new idea or method. In this context, it means they introduced a novel approach to measuring the effectiveness of image segmentation techniques."},
{"a_new_discrepancy_measure_of_segmentation_evaluation_index_sei": "The new discrepancy measure of Segmentation Evaluation Index (SEI) is a method proposed by Yang et al. to evaluate the accuracy of image segmentation. It differs from traditional methods by using a two-sided 50% overlap to redefine corresponding segments, thereby placing greater emphasis on object recognition within the segmentation assessment."},
{"segmentation_evaluation_index_sei": "The Segmentation Evaluation Index (SEI) is a novel discrepancy measure proposed by Yang et al. in 2015 to evaluate image segmentation results more accurately. It compensates for or penalizes the four basic error types identified by Gelasca, based on human visual tolerance, and places emphasis on object recognition in segmentation assessment.|Here is another description: The Segmentation Evaluation Index (SEI) is a novel discrepancy measure proposed by Yang et al. in 2015. It is designed to evaluate image segmentation results more accurately by compensating or penalizing basic error types based on human visual tolerance.|Here is another description: The Segmentation Evaluation Index (SEI) is a novel discrepancy measure proposed by Yang et al. in 2015. It redefines the corresponding segment using a two-sided 50% overlap instead of the traditional one-sided 50% overlap, aiming to make the evaluation of image segmentation results more accurate."},
{"segmentation_evaluation_index_sei__uses__a_two_sided_50_overlap": "The predicate \"uses\" in this context signifies the application or incorporation of a specific method or parameter within the Segmentation Evaluation Index (SEI). It indicates that SEI employs a particular approach to redefine the corresponding segment in image segmentation."},
{"a_two_sided_50_overlap": "A two-sided 50% overlap is a method used in the Segmentation Evaluation Index (SEI) to redefine the corresponding segment in image segmentation. Unlike the commonly used one-sided 50% overlap, this approach considers both sides of the segment, aiming to provide a more accurate evaluation of segmentation results."},
{"segmentation_evaluation_index_sei__redefines__the_corresponding_segment": "The predicate \"redefines\" indicates that the SEI introduces a new method or criteria for determining the evaluation of segments in image segmentation. Specifically, it changes the standard approach by using a two-sided 50% overlap instead of the traditional one-sided 50% overlap."},
{"the_corresponding_segment": "The \"corresponding segment\" refers to the specific portion of an image that is being evaluated for segmentation accuracy. In the context of SEI, this segment is redefined using a two-sided 50% overlap criterion, which differs from conventional methods."},
{"segmentation_evaluation_index_sei__emphasizes__the_importance_of_object_recognition_in_segmentation_assessment": "The term \"emphasizes\" in this context refers to the SEI's focus on a particular aspect of segmentation assessment. Unlike other measures, SEI places special attention on the role of object recognition within the segmentation process."},
{"the_importance_of_object_recognition_in_segmentation_assessment": "The importance of object recognition in segmentation assessment pertains to the critical role that accurately identifying and distinguishing objects plays in evaluating the quality of image segmentation. This aspect is particularly highlighted by the SEI, which integrates object recognition more effectively into its evaluation criteria."},
