{"taha_et_al": "Taha et al. refers to a group of researchers who conducted a study in 2014. Their work focused on developing methods for evaluating image segmentation techniques."},
{"taha_et_al__proposed__a_formalization_method_based_on_segmented_data_and_target_of_segmentation_task": "The predicate \"proposed\" indicates that Taha et al. put forward or suggested a new idea or method in their research. In this context, it means they introduced a novel approach for formalizing the evaluation of segmentation tasks."},
{"a_formalization_method_based_on_segmented_data_and_target_of_segmentation_task": "The object \"a formalization method based on segmented data and target of segmentation task\" describes the specific approach that Taha et al. suggested. This method involves using segmented data and the intended goal of the segmentation task to determine the most suitable metric for evaluation. It aims to measure the bias of the target attribute being assessed."},
{"proposed_method": "The proposed method refers to a formalization technique introduced by Taha et al. in 2014, which is designed to select the most appropriate metric for a segmentation task based on segmented data and the target of the segmentation.|Here is another description: The proposed method refers to a newly developed technique or approach aimed at improving the identification of over-segmentation errors (OSEs) in image segmentation tasks. It is designed to offer enhanced performance compared to existing methods.|Here is another description: The proposed method refers to a systematic approach developed by Peng and Varshney (2015) for evaluating the lower bound in the statistical estimation framework, specifically using the Mean Square Error (MSE) of segmentation algorithms as an example.|Here is another description: The proposed method refers to a systematic approach developed by Peng and Varshney (2015) for evaluating the lower bound in the statistical estimation framework, specifically using the Mean Square Error (MSE) of segmentation algorithms."},
{"proposed_method__depends_on__measuring_the_metric_bias_of_the_target_attribute_being_evaluated": "The phrase \"depends on\" indicates a necessary condition or requirement for the proposed method to function effectively. In this context, it signifies that the effectiveness or accuracy of the proposed method is contingent upon a specific process or measurement."},
{"measuring_the_metric_bias_of_the_target_attribute_being_evaluated": "Measuring the metric bias of the target attribute being evaluated involves assessing the deviation or error in the metric used for evaluating the segmentation task. This measurement is crucial for the proposed method to determine the most suitable metric for the given segmentation task."},
{"malladi_et_al": "Malladi et al. are researchers who contributed to the field of image segmentation by developing new techniques for evaluating segmentation results."},
{"malladi_et_al__proposed__a_hybrid_ground_truth_fusion_technique_for_image_segmentation_evaluation": "The predicate \"proposed\" indicates that Malladi et al. put forward or suggested a new method or technique within their research."},
{"a_hybrid_ground_truth_fusion_technique_for_image_segmentation_evaluation": "A hybrid ground truth fusion technique for image segmentation evaluation is a method designed to combine multiple ground truth references to improve the accuracy and reliability of image segmentation assessments. This technique aims to provide a more robust evaluation framework by integrating different reference images."},
{"hybrid_ground_truth_fusion_technique": "The hybrid ground truth fusion technique is a method proposed by Malladi et al. in 2018 for evaluating image segmentation. It aims to improve the accuracy and reliability of segmentation assessments by integrating multiple ground truth references."},
{"hybrid_ground_truth_fusion_technique__compared_with__existing_ground_truth_fusion_methods_on_multiple_ground_truth_datasets_with_different_roughness_levels": "The comparison involves evaluating the performance and effectiveness of the hybrid ground truth fusion technique against other established ground truth fusion methods. This assessment is conducted across various datasets that exhibit different levels of roughness, which challenges the techniques to handle complex segmentation tasks."},
{"existing_ground_truth_fusion_methods_on_multiple_ground_truth_datasets_with_different_roughness_levels": "Existing ground truth fusion methods on multiple ground truth datasets with different roughness levels represent a variety of techniques previously developed for merging multiple reference segmentations into a single, reliable ground truth. These methods are tested across datasets that vary in complexity and roughness to ensure robust performance evaluation."},
{"segmented_image__can_have__multiple_reference_images": "The phrase \"can have\" indicates a potential or possible relationship where a segmented image is not limited to a single reference but can be associated with or compared against multiple reference images. This suggests flexibility and variability in the evaluation or analysis of the segmented image."},
{"multiple_reference_images": "Multiple reference images refer to a set of images that are used as benchmarks or standards for comparing and evaluating a segmented image. These reference images provide different perspectives or versions of what the segmented image should ideally look like, allowing for a more comprehensive assessment of the segmentation quality."},
{"general_practice": "General practice in the context of image segmentation evaluation refers to the common approach or method used to assess the quality of segmented images. It involves comparing segmentation results against reference images to determine accuracy and effectiveness."},
{"general_practice__selects__the_best_one_in_many_reference_images_as_the_ground_truth": "The predicate \"selects\" indicates the action of choosing or picking the most appropriate option from a set of available choices. In this context, it describes the process of identifying the optimal reference image among multiple candidates to serve as the ground truth for evaluating segmentation results."},
{"the_best_one_in_many_reference_images_as_the_ground_truth": "The best one in many reference images as the ground truth refers to the single reference image that is deemed most accurate and representative among a collection of reference images. This selected image serves as the standard or benchmark against which segmentation results are compared to evaluate their quality and accuracy."},
{"peng_and_li": "Peng and Li are researchers who have contributed to the field of image segmentation evaluation. Their work involves developing methods to assess the quality of segmented images, focusing on structural information and adaptive evaluation techniques."},
{"peng_and_li__proposed__a_probability_measurement_method_to_evaluate_segmentation": "The predicate \"proposed\" indicates that Peng and Li introduced or put forward a new idea or method. In this context, it means they suggested a novel approach for evaluating image segmentation, specifically a probability measurement method."},
{"a_probability_measurement_method_to_evaluate_segmentation": "The object \"a probability measurement method to evaluate segmentation\" refers to the specific technique proposed by Peng and Li. This method uses probability measurements to assess the quality of image segmentation, allowing for the evaluation of structural information and the calculation of local and global similarity scores."},
{"probability_measurement_method": "The probability measurement method is a technique proposed by Peng and Li (2013) for evaluating image segmentation. It operates by assessing the quality of segmentation results through a probabilistic approach."},
{"probability_measurement_method__evaluates__the_structural_information_extracted_from_the_segmented_image": "The predicate \"evaluates\" in this context refers to the action of assessing or measuring the effectiveness or quality of the structural information derived from a segmented image using the probability measurement method."},
{"the_structural_information_extracted_from_the_segmented_image": "The structural information extracted from the segmented image represents the detailed data and features obtained after an image has been divided into segments. This information is crucial for evaluating the accuracy and quality of the segmentation process."},
{"local_similarity_score": "The local similarity score is a metric used to evaluate the similarity of each pixel in a segmented image to a reference image, providing a measure of how well the segmentation aligns with the expected or target segmentation.|Here is another description: The local similarity score refers to the measure of similarity for each individual pixel or point within a segmented image, reflecting how well each part of the segmentation aligns with the reference image."},
{"local_similarity_score__can_be_obtained__for_each_pixel_in_the_segmentation_result": "The phrase \"can be obtained\" indicates that the local similarity score is a measurable quantity that can be calculated or derived for each pixel in the segmentation result using specific evaluation methods or algorithms."},
{"for_each_pixel_in_the_segmentation_result": "For each pixel in the segmentation result refers to the individual pixels within an image that has undergone a segmentation process, where each pixel is assessed for its similarity to a reference or ground truth image."},
{"local_similarity_score__can_be_accumulated__in_the_global_similarity_score_of_the_entire_segment_by_an_information_theory_method": "The phrase \"can be accumulated\" indicates that the local similarity scores, which are calculated for each pixel or point, can be combined or summed up to form a comprehensive measure."},
{"in_the_global_similarity_score_of_the_entire_segment_by_an_information_theory_method": "The global similarity score of the entire segment, obtained through an information theory method, represents an overall measure of how well the entire segmented image matches the reference image, taking into account the accumulated local similarity scores."},
{"peng_et_al": "Peng et al. refers to a group of researchers, prominently featuring Peng, who have contributed to the field of image segmentation evaluation through various proposed methods and frameworks."},
{"peng_et_al__constructed__a_region_based_adaptive_evaluation_framework": "The term \"constructed\" indicates that Peng et al. have developed and assembled a specific framework, implying a process of design, implementation, and integration of various components to create a functional system for evaluating image segmentation."},
{"a_region_based_adaptive_evaluation_framework": "A region-based adaptive evaluation framework is a system designed by Peng et al. to assess image segmentation results. This framework does not rely on comparing segmentation results with reference segmentations but instead uses a common indicator evaluation reference, adapting to evaluate structural information extracted from segmented images using region-based metrics."},
{"region_based_adaptive_evaluation_framework": "The region-based adaptive evaluation framework is a system developed by Peng et al. (2016) to evaluate image segmentations. It adaptively constructs a common indicator evaluation reference using a new exemplary reference instead of multiple ground truth images.|Here is another description: The region-based adaptive evaluation framework is a system developed by Peng et al. (2016) to evaluate image segmentation results. It moves away from traditional methods that compare segmentation results with multiple reference segmentations."},
{"region_based_adaptive_evaluation_framework__no_longer_compares__segmentation_results_with_each_reference_segmentation": "The predicate \"no longer compares\" indicates a shift in methodology where the framework does not individually evaluate segmentation results against each reference segmentation, but rather uses a unified exemplary reference for evaluation."},
{"segmentation_results_with_each_reference_segmentation": "Segmentation results with each reference segmentation refer to the traditional approach of evaluating an image segmentation by comparing it against multiple ground truth images, assessing how well the segmentation aligns with each reference."},
{"region_based_adaptive_evaluation_framework__adaptively_builds__a_common_indicator_evaluation_reference": "The framework adaptively builds a common indicator evaluation reference, meaning it dynamically creates a standard or benchmark for evaluating segmentation results based on the specific characteristics and requirements of the segmentation task at hand."},
{"a_common_indicator_evaluation_reference": "A common indicator evaluation reference is a standardized benchmark or reference point used within the region-based adaptive evaluation framework to assess the quality of image segmentation results. This reference is not static but is adaptively generated to suit the particular segmentation task."},
{"new_example_reference": "The new example reference is a novel approach introduced in the context of image segmentation evaluation, serving as a selected reference from multiple segmentation results to adaptively build a common indicator evaluation reference."},
{"new_example_reference__is_used_with__the_general_form_of_region_based_measurement": "The phrase \"is used with\" indicates a relationship where the new example reference is employed in conjunction with another method or framework to enhance the evaluation process of image segmentation."},
{"the_general_form_of_region_based_measurement": "The general form of region-based measurement refers to a method or framework that evaluates image segmentation by considering regions within the image, rather than individual pixels, to assess the quality and accuracy of the segmentation results."},
{"this_paper__applies__three_commonly_used_region_based_metrics_pri_gce_and_voi": "The paper applies, meaning it utilizes and implements, certain metrics within its proposed framework to evaluate segmentation results effectively."},
{"three_commonly_used_region_based_metrics_pri_gce_and_voi": "The three commonly used region-based metrics (PRI, GCE, and VOI) are standard measures in the field of image segmentation for evaluating the quality and accuracy of segmentation results. These metrics assess different aspects of segmentation performance, such as region similarity, consistency, and information content."},
{"this_paper__proposes__an_effective_scheme_for_calculating_each_metric": "The predicate \"proposes\" indicates that the paper is putting forward a new idea, method, or scheme. In this context, it suggests that the paper is offering a novel approach or solution to a problem within the domain of image segmentation evaluation."},
{"an_effective_scheme_for_calculating_each_metric": "An effective scheme for calculating each metric refers to a new method introduced in the paper for computing region-based metrics such as PRI, GCE, and VOI. These metrics are crucial for evaluating the quality of image segmentation results. The scheme involves using exemplary references to calculate the values of new measurements denoted as _QPRI_, _QGCE_, and _QVOI_."},
{"proposed_new_measurements": "The proposed new measurements refer to a set of novel metrics introduced in the paper for evaluating image segmentation. These metrics are designed to improve upon existing region-based measurements by utilizing exemplary references in their calculation."},
{"proposed_new_measurements__are_denoted_as__qpri_qgce_and_qvoi": "The phrase \"are denoted as\" signifies the naming or labeling of the proposed new measurements. It indicates that specific identifiers have been assigned to these metrics to distinguish them from other measurements."},
{"qpri_qgce_and_qvoi": "_QPRI_, _QGCE_, and _QVOI_ are the designated identifiers for the proposed new measurements. These labels represent the adapted versions of the commonly used region-based metrics PRI, GCE, and VOI, respectively, which are calculated using exemplary references in the new evaluation framework."},
{"values_of_qpri_qgce_and_qvoi": "The values of _QPRI_, _QGCE_, and _QVOI_ are newly proposed measurements in the context of image segmentation evaluation, derived from commonly used region-based metrics (PRI, GCE, and VOI)."},
{"values_of_qpri_qgce_and_qvoi__are_calculated_using__only_exemplary_references": "The calculation of _QPRI_, _QGCE_, and _QVOI_ involves a specific method or formula that utilizes exemplary references to determine the values of these metrics."},
{"only_exemplary_references": "Only exemplary references are used in the calculation of _QPRI_, _QGCE_, and _QVOI_. These references serve as a standard or benchmark against which the segmentation results are evaluated, providing a basis for the adaptive evaluation framework proposed by Peng et al. (2016)."},
{"peng_et_al_2017": "Peng et al. (2017) refers to a group of researchers who authored a study in 2017, contributing to the field of image segmentation evaluation.|Here is another description: Peng et al. 2017 refers to a research publication or contribution by authors named Peng and possibly others, dated 2017. This object represents the source or origin of the introduction of _Qp_."},
{"peng_et_al_2017__came_up_with__another_framework": "The phrase \"came up with\" indicates that Peng et al. (2017) developed or proposed a new idea, method, or framework as part of their research."},
{"another_framework": "The \"another framework\" refers to a novel approach or system proposed by Peng et al. (2017) for evaluating image segmentation quality, which differs from previous methods by using multiple reference images and adaptively composing them into a final ground truth."},
{"ground_truth__is__a_combination_of_multiple_reference_images": "The predicate \"is\" in this context signifies a redefinition or an updated understanding of what constitutes the ground truth in image segmentation. It indicates a shift from a singular optimal image to a more complex and composite standard."},
{"a_combination_of_multiple_reference_images": "A combination of multiple reference images represents a new approach to defining the ground truth in image segmentation. Instead of relying on a single optimal image, this method involves aggregating the best partial segmentation results from several reference images to create a more comprehensive and robust benchmark for evaluation."},
{"best_partial_segmentation_result": "The best partial segmentation result refers to the most accurate and effective segmentation achieved within each reference image, highlighting the optimal division of the image into meaningful segments."},
{"best_partial_segmentation_result__is_labeled_and_then_adaptively_composed_into__the_final_ground_truth": "The phrase \"is labeled and then adaptively composed into\" describes a process where the best partial segmentation results from multiple reference images are identified, marked, and subsequently combined in a flexible manner to form a comprehensive and coherent final representation."},
{"the_final_ground_truth": "The final ground truth represents a composite image derived from the adaptive combination of the best partial segmentation results from various reference images, ensuring both structural consistency and local accuracy in the segmentation."},
{"final_ground_truth": "The final ground truth is a composite representation derived from the best partial segmentation results of multiple reference images, serving as a benchmark for evaluating segmentation quality.|Here is another description: The final ground truth is a composite result derived from the best partial segmentations of multiple reference images, aiming to maintain structural consistency and enhance the evaluation of segmentation quality."},
{"final_ground_truth__maintains__structural_consistency": "The predicate 'maintains' indicates that the final ground truth preserves certain qualities or characteristics throughout its composition process."},
{"structural_consistency": "Structural consistency refers to the preservation of the overall structure and integrity of the segmented objects within the final ground truth, ensuring that the composite representation remains coherent and logically consistent."},
{"final_ground_truth__locally_matches__the_input_segments": "Locally matches indicates that the final ground truth is designed to align closely with the specific details and segments of the input data, ensuring that the evaluation metric reflects the true quality of the segmentation."},
{"the_input_segments": "The input segments refer to the individual parts or sections of the image that are used as the basis for segmentation, which the final ground truth is intended to match locally for a fair and accurate evaluation."},
{"quality_of_a_given_segmentation": "The quality of a given segmentation refers to the effectiveness and accuracy of the segmentation process in dividing an image into meaningful parts. It is a critical factor in evaluating how well the segmentation aligns with the intended or expected results."},
{"quality_of_a_given_segmentation__is_measured_by__its_distance_to_the_combined_reference": "The phrase \"is measured by\" indicates the method or metric used to assess the quality of the segmentation. In this context, it specifies that the evaluation of segmentation quality is based on a particular criterion or standard."},
{"its_distance_to_the_combined_reference": "The \"distance to the combined reference\" represents the metric used to evaluate the segmentation quality. It involves calculating how far the given segmentation is from an ideal or reference segmentation that is composed of multiple optimal partial segmentations from different reference images."},
{"segmentation_results_of_multi_person_markers": "The segmentation results of multi-person markers refer to the outcomes obtained from segmenting images that contain multiple individuals, using specific markers or labels to delineate different parts or features of each person within the image."},
{"segmentation_results_of_multi_person_markers__reflect__different_levels_of_perceptual_detail": "The predicate \"reflect\" in this context signifies that the segmentation results are indicative or representative of certain characteristics or qualities. Here, it means that the segmentation outcomes show or demonstrate varying degrees of perceptual detail."},
{"different_levels_of_perceptual_detail": "Different levels of perceptual detail refer to the varying degrees of clarity, resolution, or specificity in how the segmented parts of the multi-person markers are perceived or interpreted. This can include the fineness of the segmentation, the accuracy of boundary detection, and the overall quality of the segmented regions in representing the true features of the individuals in the image."},
{"using_more_marked_segments_as_a_reference": "Using more marked segments as a reference refers to the practice of incorporating a greater number of labeled or annotated segments into the evaluation process of image segmentation quality. This approach aims to provide a more comprehensive and detailed basis for comparison."},
{"using_more_marked_segments_as_a_reference__makes__the_evaluation_fairer": "Makes indicates a causal relationship where the action or condition described by the subject leads to a specific outcome or result. In this context, it suggests that the practice of using more marked segments as a reference directly influences the fairness of the evaluation."},
{"the_evaluation_fairer": "The evaluation fairer refers to an assessment process that is more just, balanced, and unbiased. In the context of image segmentation, a fairer evaluation would more accurately reflect the true quality of the segmentation results by considering a broader range of reference points."},
{"author": "The author refers to the researcher or group of researchers who have developed and presented a novel approach to evaluating segmentation quality in image processing. In this context, the author is likely part of a research team that has identified limitations in existing evaluation metrics and sought to address them with a new method."},
{"author__proposed__a_new_algorithm_qp": "The predicate 'proposed' indicates that the author has put forward or suggested a new idea, method, or algorithm. In this case, the author has introduced a novel algorithm designed to improve the evaluation of segmentation quality, which is a significant contribution to the field of image processing and analysis."},
{"a_new_algorithm_qp": "The object 'a new algorithm (_Qp_)' represents the novel method or system that the author has developed. This algorithm is specifically designed to evaluate the quality of segmentation in images by using a ground truth based on multi-person markers, which aims to provide a more fair and robust assessment compared to traditional pixel-based analysis methods."},
{"new_algorithm_qp": "The new algorithm (_Qp_) is a novel method proposed by the author to evaluate the quality of image segmentation. It represents an advancement over traditional pixel-based evaluation metrics by incorporating more comprehensive and robust criteria.|Here is another description: The new algorithm (_Qp_) is a method proposed by Peng et al. (2017) that utilizes the ground truth of multi-person markers to assess segmentation quality. It is designed to provide a fairer evaluation by considering more marked segments as references."},
{"new_algorithm_qp__is_based_on__the_ground_truth_of_multi_person_markers": "The phrase \"is based on\" indicates that the new algorithm (_Qp_) relies on or is derived from the ground truth of multi-person markers. This means that the algorithm uses the combined reference images as a foundational element to assess segmentation quality."},
{"the_ground_truth_of_multi_person_markers": "The ground truth of multi-person markers refers to a composite reference created by adaptively combining the best partial segmentation results from multiple reference images. This ground truth maintains structural consistency and locally matches input segments, providing a fairer and more detailed basis for evaluating segmentation quality."},
{"new_algorithm_qp__evaluates__the_segmentation_quality": "The predicate \"evaluates\" signifies the action performed by the new algorithm (_Qp_) to assess or measure the quality of segmentation results. This evaluation is based on the distance of the segmentation to a combined reference composed of multiple marked segments."},
{"the_segmentation_quality": "The segmentation quality refers to the measure of how well a given segmentation aligns with the combined reference ground truth. It is assessed by the new algorithm (_Qp_) and is influenced by the structural consistency and local matching of the input segments to the reference images."},
{"most_existing_evaluation_metrics": "Most existing evaluation metrics refer to the commonly used methods and standards in the field of image segmentation quality assessment. These metrics are designed to quantitatively evaluate how well a segmentation algorithm performs by comparing its results to a ground truth or reference image."},
{"most_existing_evaluation_metrics__use__pixel_element_analysis_results": "The use of pixel element analysis results in evaluation metrics involves assessing the segmentation quality by examining individual pixels within the segmented image. This method typically calculates the percentage of pixels that correctly match the ground truth, providing a quantitative measure of segmentation accuracy."},
{"pixel_element_analysis_results": "Pixel element analysis results refer to the outcomes obtained by evaluating each pixel in a segmented image against a ground truth image. These results are used to determine the accuracy of the segmentation by identifying discrepancies between the segmented pixels and the expected pixels in the reference image."},
{"percentage_of_pixels_that_do_not_match_the_ground_truth": "The percentage of pixels that do not match the ground truth refers to the proportion of pixels in a segmentation result that deviate from the expected or ideal segmentation as defined by the ground truth. This metric is used to assess the accuracy of segmentation algorithms."},
{"percentage_of_pixels_that_do_not_match_the_ground_truth__indicates__worse_performance_of_the_segmentation_results": "The term \"indicates\" in this context signifies that a higher percentage of mismatched pixels serves as an indicator or signal of the overall quality of the segmentation results. It suggests a direct relationship between the percentage of mismatched pixels and the effectiveness of the segmentation process."},
{"worse_performance_of_the_segmentation_results": "Worse performance of the segmentation results refers to a lower quality or less accurate segmentation outcome. This can be characterized by a higher number of incorrectly segmented pixels, leading to a segmentation that does not closely match the ground truth, thereby reducing the effectiveness and reliability of the segmentation algorithm."},
{"these_methods": "These methods refer to the existing evaluation metrics that use pixel element analysis results to measure the quality of segmentation. They typically assess performance based on the percentage of pixels that match the ground truth.|Here is another description: These methods refer to the current segmentation assessment metrics that primarily focus on globally valid assessments."},
{"these_methods__are_not_always__reasonable_and_robust": "The phrase \"are not always\" indicates a condition of inconsistency or variability in the effectiveness or applicability of these methods. It suggests that while these methods can be useful, they do not consistently deliver reliable or strong results in all scenarios."},
{"reasonable_and_robust": "\"Reasonable and robust\" describes the desired qualities of evaluation methods. Reasonable implies that the methods should make sense and be logically sound in their assessments. Robust means that the methods should consistently produce reliable results even when faced with variations or challenges in the data."},
{"feng_et_al_2016": "Feng et al. (2016) refers to a research study conducted by Feng and colleagues in the year 2016, which focused on improving the evaluation metrics for image segmentation quality.|Here is another description: Feng et al. 2016 refers to a publication or research work conducted by authors named Feng and their colleagues in the year 2016. This work is likely part of a series of studies or contributions in a specific field, possibly related to image processing or analysis based on the context.|Here is another description: Feng et al. 2016, as the object, reiterates the same publication or research work by Feng and colleagues from the year 2016. This repetition emphasizes that the work itself is both the introducer and the introduced entity, highlighting its significance as a foundational or pioneering contribution in its field."},
{"feng_et_al_2016__proposed__a_new_objective_evaluation_metric_based_on_the_weighted_roc_graph": "The predicate \"proposed\" indicates that Feng et al. (2016) put forward or suggested a new idea or method as a solution to the existing problems in evaluating image segmentation quality."},
{"a_new_objective_evaluation_metric_based_on_the_weighted_roc_graph": "A new objective evaluation metric based on the weighted-ROC graph is an innovative method suggested by Feng et al. (2016) to assess the quality of image segmentation. This metric aims to address the limitations of traditional evaluation metrics by treating object pixels and background pixels differently, providing a more rational and robust assessment."},
{"new_objective_evaluation_metric": "The new objective evaluation metric is a method proposed by Feng et al. (2016) that evaluates segmentation quality based on a weighted-ROC graph, aiming to provide a more fair and robust assessment compared to traditional pixel-based metrics."},
{"new_objective_evaluation_metric__overcomes__the_irrationality_of_traditional_objective_evaluation_metric": "The predicate 'overcomes' indicates that the new objective evaluation metric addresses and resolves the limitations and flaws present in the traditional objective evaluation metric, particularly in how it handles object and background pixels."},
{"the_irrationality_of_traditional_objective_evaluation_metric": "The irrationality of the traditional objective evaluation metric refers to its inherent flaw of treating object pixels and background pixels equally, which can lead to unreasonable and non-robust segmentation evaluations."},
