{"cruz_et_al_2017": "Cruz et al. (2017) refers to a research team led by Cruz that conducted a study in 2017. Their work focused on improving the efficiency of comparing segmentation algorithms in image databases.|Here is another description: Cruz et al. 2017 refers to a study or publication by authors named Cruz and their colleagues in the year 2017. This work is likely related to research in the field of remote sensing images, as indicated by its context among other similar studies.|Here is another description: Cruz et al. 2017, as the object, is the same study or publication by authors named Cruz and their colleagues in the year 2017. It is being referred to as the entity that was introduced or presented by the subject, emphasizing its role as a foundational or innovative contribution in the context of remote sensing images."},
{"cruz_et_al_2017__realized__automatic_detection_system": "The predicate \"realized\" in this context means that Cruz et al. (2017) successfully implemented or brought into existence the automatic detection system using specific tools and methodologies."},
{"automatic_detection_system": "The automatic detection system is a computational tool developed by Cruz et al. (2017) to facilitate the comparison and analysis of segmentation algorithms. It utilizes MATLAB's parallel computing toolbox to parallelize the main part of the system, allowing for simultaneous execution and analysis of algorithms, which significantly reduces CPU usage and processing time.|Here is another description: The automatic detection system is a tool designed to expedite the comparison and evaluation of different segmentation algorithms across large image databases. It aims to enhance calculation efficiency and reduce processing time by parallelizing the main part of the system."},
{"automatic_detection_system__uses__matlab_parallel_computing_toolbox": "The predicate \"uses\" indicates the relationship where the automatic detection system incorporates or employs the MATLAB parallel computing toolbox as a means to achieve its objectives of speeding up comparisons and improving calculation efficiency."},
{"matlab_parallel_computing_toolbox": "The MATLAB parallel computing toolbox is a software tool within MATLAB that allows for the parallelization of computational tasks. It enables the simultaneous analysis and execution of algorithms, significantly reducing CPU usage and processing time compared to sequential execution."},
{"system": "The system refers to the automatic detection system developed by Cruz et al. (2017) for comparing the performance of different segmentation algorithms. This system utilizes MATLAB's parallel computing toolbox to enhance the efficiency of calculations."},
{"system__has_been__parallelized": "The phrase \"has been\" indicates a completed action where the system underwent a transformation or enhancement. In this context, it signifies that the system was modified to incorporate parallel processing capabilities."},
{"parallelized": "\"Parallelized\" describes the state of the system after it has been modified to perform multiple operations simultaneously. In this case, the main part of the system has been adapted to utilize parallel computing, allowing for the simultaneous analysis and execution of segmentation algorithms, which results in reduced CPU usage and processing time."},
{"segmentation_algorithm__can_be__analyzed_and_executed_simultaneously": "The phrase \"can be\" indicates a capability or potential state of the subject. In this context, it suggests that the segmentation algorithm has the ability or feature to be processed in a certain manner."},
{"analyzed_and_executed_simultaneously": "Being \"analyzed and executed simultaneously\" refers to the capability of the segmentation algorithm to undergo analysis and execution at the same time, rather than sequentially. This simultaneous processing can lead to increased efficiency and reduced processing time."},
{"accuracy_of_the_analysis_results": "The accuracy of the analysis results refers to the correctness and reliability of the outcomes produced by the segmentation algorithms when applied to image databases. It is a critical factor in determining the effectiveness of the algorithms in practical applications."},
{"accuracy_of_the_analysis_results__can_be__evaluated": "The phrase \"can be\" indicates a possibility or capability. In this context, it suggests that there is a method or system in place that allows for the assessment or measurement of the accuracy of the analysis results produced by the segmentation algorithms."},
{"evaluated": "The evaluation of the accuracy of the analysis results involves assessing how well the segmentation algorithms perform in terms of correctness and reliability. This evaluation can be done using various metrics and methods, such as those proposed by Su and Zhang (2017), which focus on both local and global quantization of segmentation errors."},
{"cpu_usage_and_processing_time": "CPU usage and processing time refer to the amount of computational resources consumed by a system and the duration it takes to complete a task, respectively. In the context of image segmentation algorithms, these metrics are crucial for evaluating the efficiency and performance of the system."},
{"cpu_usage_and_processing_time__is__reduced_significantly": "The predicate \"is reduced significantly\" indicates a notable decrease in the amount of CPU usage and processing time. This reduction is attributed to the implementation of parallel computing techniques, which allow for more efficient resource utilization and faster execution of tasks."},
{"reduced_significantly": "The reduction in CPU usage and processing time is a direct result of the parallelization of the segmentation algorithm. By executing multiple algorithms simultaneously and evaluating their accuracy concurrently, the system achieves a significant improvement in efficiency compared to traditional sequential execution methods."},
{"current_segmentation_assessment_metrics": "Current segmentation assessment metrics refer to the methods and criteria used to evaluate the performance of image segmentation algorithms. These metrics are crucial for determining the effectiveness of segmentation techniques in various applications."},
{"current_segmentation_assessment_metrics__focus_on__globally_valid_assessments": "The phrase \"focus on\" indicates that the current segmentation assessment metrics primarily concentrate their evaluation efforts on a specific type of assessment. This focus directs the metrics towards particular aspects or outcomes in the segmentation process."},
{"globally_valid_assessments": "Globally valid assessments in the context of segmentation refer to evaluations that consider the overall performance of segmentation algorithms across an entire image or dataset. These assessments aim to provide a comprehensive measure of how well the segmentation algorithm performs in general, without delving into local or specific region-based errors."},
{"these_methods__are_ineffective_for__two_segmentation_results_with_very_similar_overall_performance_and_very_different_local_errors": "The phrase \"are ineffective for\" indicates that the current segmentation assessment metrics do not perform well or are not suitable in certain situations."},
{"two_segmentation_results_with_very_similar_overall_performance_and_very_different_local_errors": "The situation in question involves two segmentation results that have very similar overall performance but exhibit very different local errors. This means that while the general effectiveness of the segmentations may appear comparable, the specific, localized inaccuracies within each segmentation vary significantly."},
{"su_and_zhang_2017": "Su and Zhang (2017) are researchers who contributed to the field of image segmentation by developing a novel method to assess segmentation errors."},
{"su_and_zhang_2017__proposed__a_method_for_local_and_global_quantization_segmentation_error": "The predicate \"proposed\" indicates that Su and Zhang (2017) put forward or suggested a new method or approach to address a specific problem in image segmentation."},
{"a_method_for_local_and_global_quantization_segmentation_error": "The method proposed by Su and Zhang (2017) is designed to quantify both local and global segmentation errors. It involves the quantization of over-segmentation and under-segmentation errors using the degree of region overlap, and it generates a segmentation error map to depict local indicators, with a global indicator obtained through region-weighted summation of error values."},
{"over_segmentation_and_under_segmentation_errors": "Over-segmentation and under-segmentation errors refer to the inaccuracies in image segmentation where regions are either divided into too many segments or not enough, respectively. These errors are critical in evaluating the performance of segmentation algorithms, as they directly impact the precision and effectiveness of the segmentation process."},
{"over_segmentation_and_under_segmentation_errors__are_quantized_using__the_degree_of_region_overlap": "The phrase \"are quantized using\" indicates a method or process by which the over-segmentation and under-segmentation errors are measured and converted into discrete values. This quantification allows for a more precise and comparable assessment of segmentation performance across different algorithms or conditions."},
{"the_degree_of_region_overlap": "The degree of region overlap is a metric used to quantify the extent to which segmented regions in an image correspond to the actual regions of interest. This metric is crucial for evaluating the accuracy of segmentation algorithms, as it provides a measurable indicator of how well the algorithm has performed in delineating the correct regions within an image."},
{"segmentation_error_map": "The segmentation error map is a visual representation of the errors in image segmentation, specifically designed to highlight discrepancies between the expected and actual segmentation results. It is generated by quantifying over-segmentation and under-segmentation errors using the degree of region overlap."},
{"segmentation_error_map__can_effectively_depict__local_indicators": "The phrase \"can effectively depict\" indicates that the segmentation error map has the capability to clearly and accurately represent or illustrate something. In this context, it means that the map is able to show detailed information about the segmentation errors."},
{"local_indicators": "Local indicators refer to specific, detailed measurements or markers that highlight the performance of a segmentation algorithm in particular regions of an image. These indicators are crucial for assessing the accuracy of segmentation in localized areas, rather than just providing a global assessment."},
{"global_indicator": "The global indicator is a comprehensive measure used to evaluate the overall performance of segmentation algorithms. It provides a single value that summarizes the effectiveness of a segmentation process across an entire image or dataset."},
{"global_indicator__can_be_obtained_by__region_weighted_summation_of_the_error_values_of_all_reference_segmentation_objects": "The phrase \"can be obtained by\" indicates the method or process through which the global indicator is derived. In this context, it specifies that the global indicator is calculated using a particular mathematical approach involving region-weighted summation."},
{"region_weighted_summation_of_the_error_values_of_all_reference_segmentation_objects": "The region-weighted summation of the error values of all reference segmentation objects is a method used to calculate the global indicator. This involves quantifying the over-segmentation and under-segmentation errors for each reference segmentation object using the degree of region overlap, then summing these error values with appropriate weights to obtain a single global measure of segmentation error."},
